I’ll inspect the measurement metadata and diffraction profile first, then identify candidate phases and refine them jointly. I’ll keep the structural files and quantitative results tied to the final GSAS-II project so the submitted artifacts are internally consistent.

The chemistry points toward cobalt tungstate formation, but the precursor pair can leave residual Co₃O₄ or WO₃ at 500 °C, so I won’t assume a single phase. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the locally available crystallographic search/refinement tooling before building the joint model.

The peak positions now give a clear two-phase hypothesis: monoclinic WO₃ accounts for the dominant 23–25° triplet and its higher-angle splitting, while spinel Co₃O₄ accounts for the independent series at about 19.37, 31.64, 37.20, 45.14, 59.68, and 65.56°. Both series share the same systematic specimen-position shift, which is strong evidence they are real phases rather than unmatched peaks. I’m moving to joint Rietveld refinement and will test whether lower-symmetry WO₃ or any cobalt tungstate is actually warranted by the residuals.

The first stable joint refinement gives roughly 72 wt% WO₃ and 28 wt% Co₃O₄. The shared specimen displacement refines to about −0.70 mm (the large number stored by GSAS-II is in micrometres), and both refined cells remain close to literature values. I’m still testing profile and symmetry choices because those are the main sources of uncertainty in the quantitative fraction.

The phase search is now effectively closed: adding a CoWO₄ model drives its scale to zero (a model-dependent 95% upper bound of about 0.2 wt%), and no independent residual peak series remains. I’m using the monoclinic WO₃ model because it gives the better Bragg-intensity residual and is the physically expected ambient polymorph; a pseudo-triclinic alternative changes the fractions by only about 0.3 wt%, which I’ll record as part of the model uncertainty.

The refinement is complete. I’m packaging the two-phase result now, then I’ll reopen the GSAS-II project and cross-check the CIFs, fractions, and exported profile before handing it over.

The deliverables are written. The selected model is 73.89 wt% monoclinic WO₃ and 26.11 wt% Co₃O₄; a tested CoWO₄ phase refined to zero. I’m now checking that the saved project reproduces those values and that every exported profile row and CIF is internally consistent.

Validation passed: the project reopens with exactly the two submitted phases, both CIFs re-import cleanly, all 8,301 profile points match the original observations, and the exported residual column is numerically exact. The saved refinement is converged at Rwp 3.439%.

